; Appended after closed.horn.
; Substitution and shifting leave a term unchanged when it has no variable
; they act on. `scoped ?d ?n ?t` says that every free ordinary variable of t
; is below n and every free dimension below d. shift and subst at k act only
; on ordinary variables at or above k, and dshift and dsubst at k only on
; dimensions at or above k, so for k at least the bound they return t
; itself. This is the meaning of the structural rules: by induction on the
; scoped derivation, each scope.X rule's premises give the premises of
; shift.X, subst.X, dshift.X or dsubst.X with the term unchanged, with the
; cutoff raised under a binder (scope raises n or d there too) and the
; replacement shifted (shift and dshift are defined on every term). These
; rules state that conclusion in one step, so a large term that a
; substitution does not reach is not rebuilt node by node.
(rule shift.scoped ((scoped ?d ?n ?t) (le ?n ?k)) (shift ?t ?k ?t))
(rule subst.scoped ((scoped ?d ?n ?t) (le ?n ?k)) (subst ?t ?k ?r ?t))
(rule dshift.scoped ((scoped ?d ?n ?t) (le ?d ?k)) (dshift ?t ?k ?t))
(rule dsubst.scoped ((scoped ?d ?n ?t) (le ?d ?k)) (dsubst ?t ?k ?s ?t))
